From: Brian 'redbeard' Harrington <redbeard@dead-city.org>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: ccu-k3: APBC base, framework-owned clock queries

clk: ccu-k3: APBC base, framework-owned clock queries

Add the APBC reg index to the CCU DT node and shift the probe mapping
(mpmu=0, apbc=1, apmu=2, apbs=3, rcpu=4, rcpu1..6=5..10); handle
BASE_TYPE_APBC in ccu_common_init (was falling through to the RCPU
base).

Add spacemit_ccu_get_clk_by_name() for the RPMI clock service. The
lookup matches the clock name from hw->init->name BEFORE registration
or hw->core->name AFTER: __clk_register() deliberately sets
hw->init = NULL once the clock is registered, so an init-only match
silently fails for every registered clock.

Add spacemit_ccu_clk_is_enabled() delegating to the new framework
clk_get_enabled(): the registered is_enabled op reads the gate bit
from hardware under the enable lock. The earlier implementation
checked hw->init (NULL post-registration) and fell back to reporting
every clock enabled - the gate state was fictitious. A missing
is_enabled op now maps to the conservative "enabled" (fail-safe
against re-enabling shared parents), and provider errors report
enabled rather than success.

Framework additions (components/drivers/clk/clk.c + the public
clk-provider.h):
  - clk_hw_get_ops(hw): core->ops post-registration, init->ops
    before; the only public way to reach registered ops.
  - clk_get_enabled(clk, *enabled): tri-state contract. 0 = hardware
    gate state; -RT_ENOSYS = no is_enabled op (no gate to read);
    provider errors propagate. A missing callback must NOT silently
    become "hardware verified enabled".
  - clk_has_local_set_rate(clk): capability probe only - does this
    clock own a local rate op. Does not establish that a rate change
    is safe for shared parents; callers apply their own policy.

---
